            "guidance": "Review this skill before installing or using it for production. Use test EdgeOne projects and sandbox payment credentials first, and fix or replace the payment callback verifier, auth middleware, refresh-token checks, tenant-scoped KV keys, analytics consent controls, and AI data-sharing notices before enabling real payments, customer data, cron automation, or production deployment.",
            "findings": "[SDI-4] unexpected: Artifact review confirms material security inconsistencies in sensitive paths: edge-functions/_middleware.js does not enforce JWT despite documentation, packages/payment/src/index.js accepts any parseable JSON callback as valid, and sharing/kv-keys.js drops the tenant prefix in sharded session keys.\n[PE3] unexpected: The JWT helper confirms token-type confusion: access tokens are not explicitly typed and verifyRefreshToken returns verifyJWT without requiring type='refresh', so cross-use of token classes is a real implementation flaw rather than an expected scaffold feature.\n[SQP-1] expected: Broad website-building triggers are expected for this skill, and the explicit confirmation table in SKILL.md reduces accidental high-impact execution risk; this finding is noted but not decisive by itself.\n[SQP-2] expected: Analytics, AI chat, payment secrets, and deployment credentials are expected for the advertised templates, but the artifacts under-specify consent, minimization, retention, and secret-handling controls for production use.\n[E1] expected: External AI API calls are purpose-aligned for the AI chat template and use a placeholder endpoint, but they still establish a third-party data boundary that should be disclosed and minimized before production use.\n[suspicious.env_credential_access] expected: The static scan evidence is an integration test reading BASE_URL and sending test requests to a configured or local target; this is expected for a web scaffold and does not support the Review verdict.\n[VirusTotal] expected: VirusTotal telemetry reports a benign code-insight analysis despite two engine detections; it was treated as telemetry only and was not the basis for the verdict.",
